i'm very pleased to say we're joined  once again on the programme by the  cabinet office minister michael goff mr  goff great to see you this morning um  and of course plenty to discuss as  always but i wonder if we might start  with  uh with pretty patel in the house of  commons yesterday and her announcement  uh well the beginning of the  announcement in terms of controls around  the borders  what do you make of those social  influencers who have jetted off to dubai  at nominally for work purposes at  sunning themselves  on the beach what should they have gone  in the first place for these  photo opportunities and promotional uh  concerns  no not at all  no it's just the the reason i ask is it  does feel rather similar to what boris  johnson is planning to do today  he's getting on a plane or a train he's  traveling several hundred miles all for  the purposes  of a photo opportunity he's not meeting  with the first minister or anything like  that  why is the prime minister heading to  scotland today  i think there's a world of difference  between someone who's trying to  market a product by going abroad and  putting themselves and others at risk  there's a world of difference between  that and the prime minister of the  united kingdom  in the uk uh thanking and supporting  those  on the front line who are at the heart  of our vaccination effort  uh the the prime minister as indeed  nicolas sturgeon  acknowledged in the clip that you played  uh has a responsibility and a role  to make sure that the vaccine rollout is  proceeding appropriately to thank those  on the front line  nhs professionals and those in the  british army uh who are  making sure that things work well and  it's also important that the prime  minister hears from those on the front  line  what is going well and what needs to  improve  no one would doubt the fact mr gove as  you correctly say there is a world of  difference between the cast of love  island on the beaches of dubai and the  prime minister heading to scotland  not least you know there's going to be  fewer bikini photos we certainly hope  for the state of all of our mental  health  but it is a promotional opportunity  nonetheless and nicholas sturgeon is not  suggesting  that it is improper for the prime  minister to go to scotland  what she's suggesting is that right now  with intensive care capacity where it is  with the  the new variant running rampant in the  words of the health secretary  that perhaps now not might not be the  most appropriate time  for the prime minister to do what many  of the scottish diaspora you and i  included can't do  and head home no but the prime minister  like  uh uh other ministers uh is visiting  those on the front line the prime  minister has visited those  uh in our vaccine factories who are  producing the vaccines that  are doing so much to to keep us safe the  prime minister  has visited hospitals in order to uh  thank and support those on the front  line of the nhs  and of course as as you would know neil  part of the job of any politician during  those visits as well as  supporting and thanking the people who  are doing so much to keep us safe  is also to to learn from the front line  you know sometimes politicians are  accused  of uh operating from ivory towers it is  important that all of us  learn from those on the ground uh if  there are uh things that need to be  improved in order to make sure that the  operation of our our services are more  effective  and the other thing of course is that um  when the prime minister visits other  parts of the  united kingdom uh uh other political  leaders  uh don't uh criticize him indeed there's  a welcome for the prime minister and for  other ministers  who are rolling up their sleeves and  getting in touch  uh with those on the ground or making  such a difference  um well in terms of uh the prime  minister heading to scotland clearly  this is the beginning of an effort on  the part of the government  to try and reforge the chains of union  so to speak  how is the government going to attempt  to do this particularly given where the  polling is on scottish independence  well we recognize that uh uh all the  countries of the united kingdom  uh are stronger together when we work  together  um and uh part of that as as we know is  uh the ruler of the vaccine it's the  oxford astrozenica vaccine  that has been produced in the united  kingdom and developed with the help of  our scientists  because the uk has been working together  and there have been people from scotland  wales northern ireland  and england involved in this effort  involved  in supporting our nhs um and it's also  the case  that the the the strength of the uk  treasury  its broad shoulders have been able to  provide a level of financial support to  those most in need across the united  kingdom  that reinforces how important it is for  us to stay together  um and i think you know at the moment  when we're prioritizing the fight  against the disease and also the need  for  economic recovery in due course talking  about you know changing the constitution  and so on is just a massive distraction  i i suppose the question is whether or  not the prime minister himself is is the  best person  to carry that message to the people of  scotland i mean just look at what  happened  just during prime minister's questions  yesterday he accused the scottish  government of dithering and delay when  it came to the vaccine roll out in  scotland  they haven't dithered neither have they  delayed you know he  referred once again to the scottish  nationalist party in full knowledge  that the correct name is the scottish  national party and he mr christine  jordan a lib dem mp for a member of the  snp  and he doesn't think we all look alike  does he uh no the prime minister uh  is someone who is committed heart and  soul uh to supporting the people of  scotland and the people of  uh the united kingdom uh and you know on  your point about the snp  uh they are a nationalist party uh  there's a difference between  uh nationalism but you know you know  what he's doing there you know what he's  doing there mr government he's playing  games  and i'm just saying the tone that he  strikes when he does so he's not  necessarily conducive  to telling people in scotland to remain  in the union i mean they are the largest  party in scotland they're going to  dominate at the next set of elections  and the prime minister you know thumbs  you know  sticks a thumb at them every time he  calls them the scottish nationalist  party how do you think that plays north  of gretna green  well i think for uh those who are in  the snp um who are who are not a  majority  of um uh voters in scotland uh let's uh  let's be clear about that  for those in the snp of course they want  to uh in westminster  in the house of commons make political  points and it's important that those  points are countered  but on a day-to-day basis the uk  government and the scottish government  are working uh hand-in-hand as we are  with the northern ireland executive  and the welsh government just yesterday  i had two meetings  um with the first minister of scotland  uh  cordial business-like meetings in order  to make sure that we were coordinating  our approach  towards the pandemic and that's what  happens every um  every day and every week we work  together and it's the  cooperation between the uk government  and the devolved administrations which  gives people the best of both worlds and  that is something that  uh when they're making their political  points that the snp  uh want to break up uh but  on a day-to-day basis uh that that  shared  working i think is what the the public  want to see  they want to see uh uh politicians uh  whether it's in hollywood or in  westminster or in cardiff  uh cooperating on a practical basis  to deal with the uh the medical  emergency that we all face  we are of course just a couple of days  away from the anniversary of robert  burns birth so it's perhaps appropriate  to quote what some power the gifty gears  to see ourselves  as other seers boris johnson doesn't  need divine intervention to know what  the people of scotland think about him  the polling shows  that 79 of swing voters in scotland  don't want boris johnson to leave the  country so again  i ask the question is boris johnson the  best front man for the union  yes he's the prime minister of the whole  united kingdom and it's important  to recognize that uh the case for the uk  the case for us working together uh is  uh  uh the most important uh uh point of  of of any debate uh when we're looking  at uh how we respond to this pandemic  when we're looking at how we respond to  uh the economy and uh it is the case  that  it is boris prime minister uh who has  been at the heart of making sure  that the uk is leading the world in  terms of vaccine development and vaccine  rollout it's also the case that uh boris  prime minister  has put in place working with rishi  sunek the economic support that is  helping people  in scotland today as it is helping  people across the united kingdom  and the important thing i think is to  look at the  uh the day-to-day reality of the uh uh  the combined strength  uh that the united kingdom brings to  meeting all of these challenges  sure on the topic of vaccines can we can  we discuss the situation as regards your  your colleague um sir desmond swain what  what what message  is desmond sending would he urge his  anti-vaccination campaigners to  to keep going with their fight against  government restrictions  he told them that nhs capacity figures  were being manipulated to exaggerate the  scale of coronavirus he even said that  the government was attempting to  implement social control  uh with his masks policy might you you  know bring your skills of  your powers considerable powers of  persuasion to bear on sir desmond and  tell him to shut up right now  uh so desmond is wrong um you know i  i've worked with sir desmond um i have  great uh  affection for him uh but i'm afraid here  he's completely  out of order is there not though a case  given what we saw in wrexham where the  fit and fill  takes place for the astrazeneca uh  vaccine we saw  someone sent an inert device but a  device to the factory there presumably  from the anti-vaccine community  given that given the importance of the  vaccine campaign  is it not possible is it not is it not  perfectly expectable  that sir desmond should have the whip  removed well i i'd say two things  uh the first thing is uh let's separate  what happened in the in the factory  that's going to be investigated  appropriately  um but uh in the case of what sir  desmond said  uh i would hope that he would uh uh  issue a full and complete  retraction and apology for what he said  it's unacceptable  absent that he should lose the whip then  well  i think that uh the first thing that  should happen is that uh so desmond  should  apologize um and then of course uh  consideration can be given to  any other steps that might be taken but  i think it's important that uh  uh so desmond has a chance to uh retract  withdraw and say sorry for these  comments which as i said  i mean but i'll repeat perhaps the the  gravest charge against him is the fact  that he  suggests that capacity figures within  the health service  are being manipulated he is accusing the  chief medical  the chief medical officer the chief  scientific advisor the legions of people  who work at the cabin office and  elsewhere in whitehall at the moment the  people who put this data together  he's accusing them of lying well  uh he's wrong and he should say sorry  well and again but i will go back to it  but absent that apology  absent the full retraction so desmond  has no place in the conservative party  presumably  i think it's for so desmond to um make  that apology to retract and to  withdraw his words um and i'm sure uh he  will  reflect on that mistake and i'm sure  that uh he will uh be  very clear that it was a serious mistake  and  an apology will be forthcoming and mr  gove i know we have to let you go in a  moment but i just want to ask you again  on on the borders  why have we not introduced a a blanket  rule  looking at the 30 countries on the hot  list one of them not in it  is mexico currently running fourth in  the world for total deaths  17 000 new cases yesterday the best part  of 2000  deaths it rather makes a mockery of the  red list if a country like mexico is not  on it  this morning we'll be uh reviewing uh  not just which countries should be  subject to these new quarantine measures  but also  as you know there are some exemptions  for certain professions will be  reviewing those exemptions  because we want to have as tight a  policy as possible

